/*
 * Linker version script for libgraphics.so.  ONLYOFFICE/DesktopEditors#2136.
 *
 * libgraphics compiles FreeType 2.10.4 (freetype.pri), harfbuzz and brotli straight
 * into itself, and nothing in the tree passes -fvisibility=hidden or a version script,
 * so every FT_*, hb_* and Brotli* symbol was exported with default visibility.
 *
 * ELF resolves symbols through one process-wide scope in load order, and desktop-apps
 * links -lgraphics ahead of the GTK stack. So the FT_* calls cairo and pango make while
 * painting a file name bound to libgraphics' 2.10.4 copy - but only the ones it
 * defines. The COLRv1 colour-glyph API arrived in FreeType 2.11 and is absent from
 * 2.10.4, so an emoji's colour path fell through to the system FreeType 2.13, which
 * then read a face laid out by 2.10.4. That is the segfault in #2136: emoji only, and
 * only with a font installed that sends the name down the colour path, which is why a
 * clean VM could not reproduce it.
 *
 * Marking these local keeps the bundled copies to ourselves. Calls from inside
 * libgraphics still resolve normally - fontengine/ and agg-2.4/font_freetype are
 * compiled into this same library and are the only callers in the tree - while
 * cairo, pango and everything else in the process get the system FreeType they were
 * built against.
 *
 * Deliberately a list of what to hide rather than a whitelist of what to export. The
 * public surface here is large (NSFonts, Aggplus, asc*, the metafile and raster
 * entry points) and enumerating it risks hiding something a caller needs, which would
 * show up as a link or runtime failure elsewhere. Missing a third-party prefix in this
 * list only leaves that prefix as exposed as it already was.
 *
 * Verify after building:  nm -D --defined-only libgraphics.so | grep -c ' T FT_'
 * must print 0.
 */
{
  local:
    FT_*;
    ft_*;
    hb_*;
    _hb_*;
    Brotli*;
};
